"By about 1500 BCE, Maya people in southern Mexico and Central America were living in villages and growing corn, beans, and squash. By 200 CE their ceremonial centers had become cities with temples, pyramids, palaces, ball courts, and plazas. They cut limestone blocks with tools of harder stone, such as chert. In the Classic Period, from about 250 to 900, there were more than 40 Maya cities, among them Tikal, Copán, and Calakmul.": "cf71de9e8cf2",
"By about 1500 BCE, Maya people in southern Mexico and Central America were living in villages and growing corn, beans, and squash. By 200 CE their ceremonial centers had become cities with temples, pyramids, palaces, ball courts, and plazas. They cut limestone blocks with tools of harder stone, such as chert. In the Classic Period, from about 250 to 900, there were more than 40 Maya cities, among them Tikal, Copán, and Calakmul. The Maya developed a full writing system; Britannica calls it the only true writing system of the Americas before Europeans arrived. They carved inscriptions on stone monuments and painted books, called codices, on paper made from fig-tree bark. They counted in twenties, with a sign for zero, and kept careful calendars and tables of the Moon and Venus. After about 900, many cities of the southern lowlands emptied, while cities in the Yucatán, such as Chichén Itzá, went on. The Maya people did not disappear: more than five million people speak some 30 Mayan languages today. The Aztecs, who called themselves the Mexica, spoke Nahuatl. Their tradition says they wandered until they saw an eagle on a cactus on a small island in Lake Texcoco, and there, about 1325, they founded Tenochtitlan. They built causeways to the shore and farmed chinampas, narrow fields built up from lake mud that gave several harvests a year. By 1519 the Aztecs and their allies ruled some 5 to 6 million people, who paid tribute to the capital. The Inca made their capital at Cusco, high in the Andes. From 1438, under the ruler Pachacuti and his successors, they conquered an empire that stretched from what is now Ecuador to central Chile. They had no writing in the European sense; officials kept records on khipus, cords with knots that stood for numbers. They built a road network of more than 30,000 km, partly on older roads, with rope bridges over the gorges, and relay runners carried messages along it. These peoples built without wheeled carts and without horses or oxen. Wheels existed in Mesoamerica, but only on small clay figures. People carried loads themselves, and in the Andes llamas carried them too. The Maya cut limestone with tools of harder stone, and Inca builders cut stone so precisely that their walls still stand without mortar. In 1519 Spanish soldiers led by Hernán Cortés reached Mexico. With thousands of Indigenous allies who resented Aztec rule, and with diseases the people had never met, they took Tenochtitlan in 1521. In 1532 Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca ruler Atahuallpa at a time of civil war, and the Spanish took Cusco in 1533. Many of the first written accounts of these peoples were made by the conquerors and missionaries, so historians read them carefully, next to archaeology and the peoples' own records.": "2932e1f42454",
